Looking at the diff: `isPlainObject` used to return `true` only for objects whose prototype is `null` or `Object.prototype`; the patched version returns `true` for **any** non-null, non-array object.

`isPlainObject` is the `identify` function of `mapTag` (and `realMapTag`/`legacyMapTag`). In the dumper (`build` → `matchTag` → `buildRepresentTypes`), the last-resort tag for any value is `mapTag`. With the original code, an object with a custom prototype (e.g., a class instance) is not identified by any tag, so `dump()` throws `YAMLException: unacceptable kind of an object to dump [object Object]`. With the patched code, `isPlainObject` accepts it, so the same call silently serializes the instance as a `!!map`. That is an observable behaviour change (it also silently changes `!!omap` item acceptance on load, though that path is harder to reach through public `load`). The change is not an equivalent refactor.

A test asserting the original (correct) behaviour — that dumping a non-plain object is rejected — passes on the unpatched build and fails on the patched one.

